---

```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a VLAN bridge membership misconfiguration on switch1 affecting exclusively the port serving PC5. On switch1, the Linux bridge implementation represents each VLAN as a separate bridge instance: br_vlan1 (native/untagged VLAN 1) and br_vlan10 (VLAN 10, the Campus 1 workstation subnet). All three working ports — eth3 (PC6), eth4 (PC7), and eth5 (PC8) — are correctly enslaved to br_vlan10. However, eth2 (the access port for PC5) is enslaved to br_vlan1 instead of br_vlan10. As a result, every frame originating from PC5 is forwarded into the wrong bridge domain. Bridged frames from PC5 never reach any VLAN 10 member, so ARP requests for 172.16.10.254 (the default gateway) and for peer hosts (PC6–PC8) are dropped without response. The link layer is fully operational (eth2 is UP/LOWER_UP), which is why PC5 reports carrier; the failure is purely at the bridge-membership/VLAN assignment level. The root cause is that switch1's startup script assigned eth2 to br_vlan1 instead of br_vlan10, isolating PC5 into the native VLAN while leaving all other VLAN 10 access ports correctly configured. The corrective fix is to reassign eth2: run `ip link set eth2 nomaster && ip link set eth2 master br_vlan10` on switch1 (and correct the startup script so the assignment survives a restart).
=== END ===
```